
[38;5;231m⏺[39m Got it — both, with a shared core.

  [1mNext fork: where the browser logs actually land.[0m This is the decision that makes or breaks "debug production issues,"
  and it's the one with a real dependency attached. Worth flagging: there's no server in this repo. [38;5;153mapp.js[39m is a stub and
  [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m points at [38;5;153mapi.example.com[39m, so I can't assume there's a backend of yours to POST to.

  - [1mYour own collector endpoint[0m — a [38;5;153mPOST /logs[39m you own. No vendor, no data leaving your infrastructure, full control of
    retention. Costs you a service to build and operate, and this repo has none, so that's net-new work outside the
    current codebase. Hardest to change later because the log schema becomes yours to version.
  - [1mHosted service (Sentry / Datadog / Logtail / similar)[0m — working error capture, stack traces, search, and alerting on
    day one, plus session context you'd otherwise hand-roll. Costs a third-party dependency and sends user data to a
    vendor, which needs a privacy sign-off. Cheap to swap out later [3mif[0m the shared core keeps the vendor behind a sink
    interface, which is exactly what the shared core buys you.
  - [1mBrowser-local buffer only[0m — a capped ring buffer in [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m plus an "export diagnostics" button. No infra, no
    data transmission, no privacy exposure. But it's pull-not-push: you only see logs when a user hands them to you,
    which doesn't help for issues nobody reports.

  My recommendation is the hosted service behind a sink interface. It's the only option that gets you real production
  visibility without building and operating a log service, and the shared core means swapping to your own collector
  later is a one-file change rather than a rewrite.
